WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.970
Strike is the direction of a horizontal line on an inclined

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:07.730
plane. Dip is the direction and angle of steepest descent of

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:11.950
that plane. Slip may be along dip, along strike, or oblique.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:15.630
Plane orientation and movement direction answer different questions.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.970
For an inclined fault, the hanging wall is the block above

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.010
the plane and the footwall lies below it.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.070
The labels follow the fault plane;

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.910
they are not assigned by which block appears higher on the

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:27.630
modern landscape.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.670
On a normal fault the hanging wall moves down relative to

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.550
the footwall, commonly during extension.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.330
On a reverse fault it moves up, commonly during shortening;

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.190
a thrust is a low-angle reverse fault.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.390
Strike-slip faults have predominantly horizontal movement.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.870
A graben is a block that has dropped relative to blocks

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.030
on either side along normal faults.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.010
A horst is relatively raised.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:55.330
These are relative displacements within a fault system, so present elevation

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:58.410
alone does not define every block’s complete history.
